      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-182166",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following sequence that diverges to $- \u221e$:\n\n$a_{n} = 5 - 2n, n \\ge 1$\n\nLet $M < 0$. We wish to find a natural number $N(M)$ such that for all $n \\ge N$, we have $a_{n} < M$.\n\nComplete the reasoning below, and hence find a suitable $N$ that satisfies this condition.\n\nFirst, notice that $5 - 2n < 0$ for any natural {{field-1}}.\n\nSo, we solve the inequality $a_{n} < M$ for\n{{field-2}}:\n\n$M - 5$ {{field-3}} {{field-4}}\n\nThen, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$5 - M$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}\n\nTherefore, $5 - 2n$\n{{field-7}}\nfor all natural numbers\n{{field-8}}."
    },
